 ## Overview
-Simplified Dual Contouring is an isosurface extraction method that generates a mesh by placing a single vertex at the center of every voxel cell where the scalar field exhibits a sign change along any axis-aligned edge, and constructing a quad mesh by connecting these vertices based on their relative grid positions [S1, unofficial]. Unlike standard Dual Contouring, which minimizes a Quadratic Error Function (QEF) to place vertices optimally within cells, this method uses a fixed geometric placement (cell center) and fixed connectivity rules, resulting in a simpler but potentially less accurate or topologically robust mesh [S1, unofficial]. It builds on [Dual Contouring](778-dual-contouring.md) by removing the QEF solver and complex topology handling in favor of a direct grid-based quad construction.
